INFO:     [82] Biography and Scientific Contributions of Marion King Hubbert --[REFERENCES]--> marion king hubbert
INFO:     [83] He proposed a constitutive equation for absolute permeability.
INFO:     [84] Hubbert was a member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences.
INFO:     [85] His papers are at the American Heritage Center.
INFO:     [86] He presented a paper to the 1956 meeting of the American Petroleum Institute.
INFO:     [87] He received the Arthur L. Day Medal in 1954.
INFO:     [88] The equation includes average grain diameter as a variable D.
INFO:     [89] He received a Bachelor of Science in 1926.
INFO:     [90] He predicted petroleum production would resemble a bell curve.
INFO:     [91] Hubbert's work is related to Bioeconomics (biology).
INFO:     [92] Hubbert was a member of the board of governors.
INFO:     [93] He believed nuclear energy in breeder reactors would sustain humanity.
INFO:     [94] He demonstrated that rock exhibits plasticity similar to clay.
INFO:     [95] He taught geophysics at Columbia University.
INFO:     [96] Hubbert proposed a constitutive equation for absolute permeability.
INFO:     [97] He showed that Darcy's law can be derived from the Navier-Stokes equation of motion.
INFO:     [98] The equation includes a dimensionless proportionality constant N.
INFO:     [99] His model ties reserves, discovery rates, and production rates.
INFO:     [100] He received a Doctor of Philosophy in 1937.
INFO:     [101] He made a mathematical demonstration that rock in the earth's crust should exhibit plasticity.
INFO:     [102] He received the Elliott Cresson Medal in 1981.
INFO:     [103] His model has been widely applied to other finite resources.
INFO:     [104] Hubbert presented a force potential that bears his name.
INFO:     [105] He believed solar power would replace fossil fuels.
INFO:     [106] Hubbert is listed with Fred Meissner in the See also section.
INFO:     [107] He received the Vetlesen Prize from the G. Unger Vetlesen Foundation.
INFO:     [108] He was long affiliated with the Geological Society of America.
INFO:     [109] He made important contributions to geology.
INFO:     [110] Hubbert projected that global oil production would peak in 1995.
INFO:     [111] He co-founded Technocracy Incorporated with Howard Scott.
INFO:     [112] He notably developed the Hubbert curve.
INFO:     [113] He believed nuclear energy could sustain humanity for centuries.
INFO:     [114] Marion King Hubbert is also referred to as King Hubbert.
INFO:     [115] He received a Master of Science in 1928.
INFO:     [116] He studied mathematics during his studies.
INFO:     [117] His contributions include prediction of migration paths of hydrocarbons.
INFO:     [118] Hubbert was a member of the National Academy of Sciences.
INFO:     [119] Hubbert is best known for his studies on the size of natural gas reserves.
INFO:     [120] He showed that Darcy's law can be derived from the Navier-Stokes equation of motion.
INFO:     [121] He believed nuclear energy in breeder reactors would be able to sustain humanity.
INFO:     [122] Hubbert is best known for his studies on the size of oil fields.
INFO:     [123] He received the Society's Penrose Medal in 1973.
INFO:     [124] He predicted that the rate of petroleum production would resemble a bell curve.
INFO:     [125] He studied physics during his studies.
INFO:     [126] He was elected President of the Society in 1962.
INFO:     [127] His model ties reserves, discovery rates, and production rates.
INFO:     [128] Hubbert died while receiving treatment for pulmonary embolism.
INFO:     [129] Hubbert was born in San Saba.
INFO:     [130] He served as secretary of education in that organization.
INFO:     [131] Hubbert believed that solar power would be a practical renewable energy replacement for fossil fuels.
INFO:     [132] He co-founded Technocracy Incorporated with Howard Scott.
INFO:     [133] Hubbert wrote a study course called the Technocracy Study Course.
INFO:     [134] Hubbert was an avid technocrat.
INFO:     [135] He also studied the flow of underground fluids.
INFO:     [136] His proposal was for an underground water or oil reservoir.
INFO:     [137] He predicted that overall petroleum production would peak in the United States.
INFO:     [138] He attended the University of Chicago.
INFO:     [139] M. King Hubbert papers are at the University of Wyoming.
INFO:     [140] He stated uranium would be an adequate energy supply for the foreseeable future.
INFO:     [141] He received the Vetlesen Prize in 1981.
INFO:     [142] He showed Darcy's law can be derived from the Navier-Stokes equation of motion of a viscous fluid.
INFO:     [143] His bibliography is archived on the Wayback Machine.
INFO:     [144] Media related to Marion King Hubbert is at Wikimedia Commons.
INFO:     [145] Quotations related to M. King Hubbert are at Wikiquote.
